---
id: import-protection
title: Import Protection
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Experimental:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Import protection is experimental and subject to change."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Import protection prevents server-only code from leaking into client bundles and client-only code from leaking into server bundles. It runs inside TanStack Start and is enabled by default."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"how-it-works","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How It Works"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Start builds your application for two environments: "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"client"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"server"}]},{"type":"text","value":". Some code should only run in one environment. Import protection checks every import in your source files during development and build, and either blocks or mocks imports that cross environment boundaries."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"There are two ways an import can be denied:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"File patterns"}]},{"type":"text","value":" match on the resolved file path. By default, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"*.server.*"},{"type":"text","value":" files are denied in the client environment and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"*.client.*"},{"type":"text","value":" files are denied in the server environment."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Specifier patterns"}]},{"type":"text","value":" match on the raw import string. By default, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/react-start/server"},{"type":"text","value":" is denied in the client environment."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"default-rules","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Default Rules"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Import protection is enabled out of the box with these defaults:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Setting"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Default"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"behavior"},{"type":"text","value":" (dev)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'mock'"},{"type":"text","value":" -- warn and replace with a mock module"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"behavior"},{"type":"text","value":" (build)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'error'"},{"type":"text","value":" -- fail the build"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"log"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'once'"},{"type":"text","value":" -- deduplicate repeated violations"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scope"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Files inside Start's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"srcDirectory"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client environment denials:"}]}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Files matching "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"**/*.server.*"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The specifier "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/react-start/server"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Excluded from file checks: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"**/node_modules/**"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server environment denials:"}]}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Files matching "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"**/*.client.*"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Excluded from file checks: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"**/node_modules/**"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By default, files inside "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"node_modules"},{"type":"text","value":" are excluded from resolved-target deny checks via the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"excludeFiles"},{"type":"text","value":" option. This prevents false positives from third-party packages whose resolved filenames contain "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".client."},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".server."},{"type":"text","value":". If you need to check third-party files, set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"excludeFiles: []"},{"type":"text","value":" on the relevant environment — see "},{"type":"link","href":"#configuring-deny-rules","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Configuring Deny Rules"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"These defaults mean you can use the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".server.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".client.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" naming convention to restrict files to a single environment without any configuration. To also deny entire directories (e.g. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"server/"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"client/"},{"type":"text","value":"), add them via "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"files"},{"type":"text","value":" in your "},{"type":"link","href":"#configuring-deny-rules","children":[{"type":"text","value":"deny rules configuration"}]},{"type":"text","value":" — for example "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"files: ['**/*.server.*', '**/server/**']"},{"type":"text","value":" for the client environment."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"type-only-imports","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Type-Only Imports"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Type-only imports and re-exports are ignored by import protection because they are erased from the runtime bundle and cannot leak environment-specific code."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import type { User } from './db.server'\nimport { type RequestHandler } from '@tanstack/react-start/server'\n\nexport type { User } from './db.server'","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mixed imports still count when they include at least one runtime value. Split the type and value imports if only the type is safe to cross the environment boundary."}]},{"type":"code","value":"// This is still checked because `getUsers` is a runtime value.\nimport { type User, getUsers } from './db.server'","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"file-markers","children":[{"type":"text","value":"File Markers"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can explicitly mark a module as server-only or client-only by adding a side-effect import at the top of the file:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// src/lib/secrets.ts\nimport '@tanstack/react-start/server-only'\n\nexport const API_KEY = process.env.API_KEY","lang":"ts"},{"type":"code","value":"// src/lib/local-storage.ts\nimport '@tanstack/react-start/client-only'\n\nexport function savePreferences(prefs: Record<string, string>) {\n  localStorage.setItem('prefs', JSON.stringify(prefs))\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When the plugin sees a marker import, it records the file as restricted. If that file is later imported from the wrong environment, the import is denied. Both markers in the same file is always an error."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Markers are useful when a file doesn't follow the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".server.*"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".client.*"},{"type":"text","value":" naming convention but still contains environment-specific code."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"behavior-modes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Behavior Modes"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"behavior"},{"type":"text","value":" option controls what happens when a violation is detected:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'error'"}]},{"type":"text","value":" -- The build fails with a detailed error message. This is the default for production builds."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'mock'"}]},{"type":"text","value":" -- The import is replaced with a mock module that returns safe proxy values. A warning is logged but the build continues. This is the default during development."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mock mode is useful during development because it lets you keep working even when your import graph has violations. The mock module returns a recursive Proxy, so any property access or function call on a mocked import returns another mock instead of crashing."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can override the defaults:"}]},{"type":"component","name":"tabs","attributes":{"variant":"bundler"},"children":[{"type":"component","name":"tab-panel","tagName":"md-tab-panel","attributes":{},"properties":{"data-tab-slug":"vite","data-tab-index":"0","data-content":"code-only"},"children":[{"type":"code","value":"import { defineConfig } from 'vite'\nimport { tanstackStart } from '@tanstack/react-start/plugin/vite'\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  plugins: [\n    tanstackStart({\n      importProtection: {\n        // Always error, even in dev\n        behavior: 'error',\n      },\n    }),\n  ],\n})","lang":"ts","meta":"title=\"vite.config.ts\"","title":"vite.config.ts","file":"vite.config.ts"}]},{"type":"component","name":"tab-panel","tagName":"md-tab-panel","attributes":{},"properties":{"data-tab-slug":"rsbuild","data-tab-index":"1","data-content":"code-only"},"children":[{"type":"code","value":"import { defineConfig } from '@rsbuild/core'\nimport { tanstackStart } from '@tanstack/react-start/plugin/rsbuild'\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  plugins: [\n    tanstackStart({\n      importProtection: {\n        // Always error, even in dev\n        behavior: 'error',\n      },\n    }),\n  ],\n})","lang":"ts","meta":"title=\"rsbuild.config.ts\"","title":"rsbuild.config.ts","file":"rsbuild.config.ts"}]}],"properties":{"data-attributes":"{\"tabs\":[{\"slug\":\"vite\",\"name\":\"vite\"},{\"slug\":\"rsbuild\",\"name\":\"rsbuild\"}]}","data-bundler-meta":"{\"bundlers\":[\"vite\",\"rsbuild\"]}"}},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Or set different behaviors per mode:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"importProtection: {\n  behavior: {\n    dev: 'mock',\n    build: 'error',\n  },\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"configuring-deny-rules","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Configuring Deny Rules"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can add your own deny rules on top of the defaults. Rules are specified per environment using glob patterns (via "},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/micromatch/picomatch","children":[{"type":"text","value":"picomatch"}]},{"type":"text","value":") or regular expressions."}]},{"type":"component","name":"tabs","attributes":{"variant":"bundler"},"children":[{"type":"component","name":"tab-panel","tagName":"md-tab-panel","attributes":{},"properties":{"data-tab-slug":"vite","data-tab-index":"0","data-content":"code-only"},"children":[{"type":"code","value":"import { defineConfig } from 'vite'\nimport { tanstackStart } from '@tanstack/react-start/plugin/vite'\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  plugins: [\n    tanstackStart({\n      importProtection: {\n        client: {\n          // Block specific npm packages from the client bundle\n          specifiers: ['@prisma/client', 'bcrypt'],\n          // Block files in a custom directory\n          files: ['**/db/**'],\n        },\n        server: {\n          // Block browser-only libraries from the server\n          specifiers: ['localforage'],\n        },\n      },\n    }),\n  ],\n})","lang":"ts","meta":"title=\"vite.config.ts\"","title":"vite.config.ts","file":"vite.config.ts"}]},{"type":"component","name":"tab-panel","tagName":"md-tab-panel","attributes":{},"properties":{"data-tab-slug":"rsbuild","data-tab-index":"1","data-content":"code-only"},"children":[{"type":"code","value":"import { defineConfig } from '@rsbuild/core'\nimport { tanstackStart } from '@tanstack/react-start/plugin/rsbuild'\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  plugins: [\n    tanstackStart({\n      importProtection: {\n        client: {\n          // Block specific npm packages from the client bundle\n          specifiers: ['@prisma/client', 'bcrypt'],\n          // Block files in a custom directory\n          files: ['**/db/**'],\n        },\n        server: {\n          // Block browser-only libraries from the server\n          specifiers: ['localforage'],\n        },\n      },\n    }),\n  ],\n})","lang":"ts","meta":"title=\"rsbuild.config.ts\"","title":"rsbuild.config.ts","file":"rsbuild.config.ts"}]}],"properties":{"data-attributes":"{\"tabs\":[{\"slug\":\"vite\",\"name\":\"vite\"},{\"slug\":\"rsbuild\",\"name\":\"rsbuild\"}]}","data-bundler-meta":"{\"bundlers\":[\"vite\",\"rsbuild\"]}"}},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"checking-third-party-packages","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Checking third-party packages"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By default, resolved files inside "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"node_modules"},{"type":"text","value":" are excluded from resolved-target deny checks (file-pattern and marker checks). This avoids false positives from packages that happen to use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".client."},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".server."},{"type":"text","value":" in their distribution filenames. If you want to re-enable checking for a specific environment, set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"excludeFiles"},{"type":"text","value":" to an empty array:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"importProtection: {\n  server: {\n    // Re-enable file-pattern checking for node_modules in the server environment\n    excludeFiles: [],\n  },\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When you provide "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"excludeFiles"},{"type":"text","value":", it "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"fully replaces"}]},{"type":"text","value":" the default ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"['**/node_modules/**']"},{"type":"text","value":"). To exclude additional paths while still skipping "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"node_modules"},{"type":"text","value":", include both:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"importProtection: {\n  client: {\n    excludeFiles: ['**/node_modules/**', '**/vendor/**'],\n  },\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"scoping-and-exclusions","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scoping and Exclusions"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By default, import protection only checks files inside Start's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"srcDirectory"},{"type":"text","value":". You can change the scope with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"include"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"exclude"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ignoreImporters"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"importProtection: {\n  // Only check files matching these patterns\n  include: ['src/**'],\n  // Skip checking these files\n  exclude: ['src/generated/**'],\n  // Ignore violations when these files are the importer\n  ignoreImporters: ['**/*.test.ts', '**/*.spec.ts'],\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"reading-violation-traces","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reading Violation Traces"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When a violation is detected, the plugin shows a diagnostic message with the full import chain that led to the violation, a code snippet highlighting the offending line, and actionable suggestions."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"server-only-code-in-the-client","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server-only code in the client"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This example shows a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"*.server.*"},{"type":"text","value":" file being imported transitively in the client environment:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"[import-protection] Import denied in client environment\n\n  Denied by file pattern: **/*.server.*\n  Importer: src/features/auth/session.ts:5:27\n  Import: \"../db/queries.server\"\n  Resolved: src/db/queries.server.ts\n\n  Trace:\n    1. src/routes/index.tsx:2:34 (entry) (import \"../features/auth/session\")\n    2. src/features/auth/session.ts:5:27 (import \"../db/queries.server\")\n\n  Code:\n     3 | import { logger } from '../utils/logger'\n     4 |\n  >  5 | import { getUsers } from '../db/queries.server'\n       |                           ^\n     6 |\n     7 | export function loadAuth() {\n\n  src/features/auth/session.ts:5:27\n\n  Suggestions:\n    - Wrap in createServerFn().handler(() => ...) to make it callable from the client via RPC\n    - Wrap in createServerOnlyFn(() => ...) if it should not be callable from the client\n    - Use createIsomorphicFn().client(() => ...).server(() => ...) for environment-specific implementations\n    - Split the file so client-safe exports are separate","lang":"text"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"client-only-code-on-the-server","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client-only code on the server"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This example shows a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"*.client.*"},{"type":"text","value":" file imported in the SSR environment. Because the code snippet contains JSX, the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"<ClientOnly>"},{"type":"text","value":" suggestion is shown first:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"[import-protection] Import denied in server environment\n\n  Denied by file pattern: **/*.client.*\n  Importer: src/components/dashboard.tsx:3:30\n  Import: \"./browser-widget.client\"\n  Resolved: src/components/browser-widget.client.tsx\n\n  Trace:\n    1. src/routes/dashboard.tsx:1:32 (entry) (import \"../components/dashboard\")\n    2. src/components/dashboard.tsx:3:30 (import \"./browser-widget.client\")\n\n  Code:\n     1 | import { BrowserWidget } from './browser-widget.client'\n     2 |\n  >  3 | export function Dashboard() { return <BrowserWidget /> }\n       |                              ^\n     4 |\n\n  src/components/dashboard.tsx:3:30\n\n  Suggestions:\n    - Wrap in <ClientOnly fallback={...}>...</ClientOnly> to render only after hydration\n    - Wrap in createClientOnlyFn(() => ...) if it should only run in the browser\n    - Use createIsomorphicFn().client(() => ...).server(() => ...) for environment-specific implementations\n    - Split the file so server-safe exports are separate","lang":"text"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"how-to-read-the-output","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How to read the output"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each violation message has these sections:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Section"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Description"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Header"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Environment type where the violation occurred ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"\"client\""},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"\"server\""},{"type":"text","value":")"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Denied by"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The rule that matched: file pattern, specifier pattern, or marker"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Importer / Import / Resolved"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The importing file (with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"file:line:col"},{"type":"text","value":"), the raw import string, and the resolved target path"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Trace"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The full import chain from the entry point to the denied import. Each step shows "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"file:line:col"},{"type":"text","value":" and the import specifier used. Step 1 is always the entry point"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Code"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A source code snippet with a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":">"},{"type":"text","value":" marker on the offending line and a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"^"},{"type":"text","value":" caret pointing to the exact column"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Suggestions"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Actionable steps to fix the violation, tailored to the direction (server-in-client vs client-in-server)"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The trace reads top-to-bottom, from the entry point to the denied module. This helps you find where the chain starts so you can restructure your code."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"common-pitfall-why-some-imports-stay-alive","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Common Pitfall: Why Some Imports Stay Alive"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"It can look like Start \"should have removed that server-only import\". The important detail is that this is handled by the Start compiler:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The compiler rewrites environment-specific "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"implementations"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for the current target (client or server)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"As part of that compilation, it prunes code and removes imports that become unused after the rewrite."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In practice, when the compiler replaces a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createServerFn()"},{"type":"text","value":" handler with a client RPC stub, it can also remove server-only imports that were only used by the removed implementation."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example (client build):"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { getUsers } from './db/queries.server'\nimport { createServerFn } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nexport const fetchUsers = createServerFn().handler(async () => {\n  return getUsers()\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Conceptually, the client build output becomes something like (simplified):"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createClientRpc } from '@tanstack/react-start/client-rpc'\nimport { createServerFn } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\n// Compiler replaces the handler with a client RPC stub.\n// (The id is generated by the compiler; treat it as an opaque identifier.)\nexport const fetchUsers = TanStackStart.createServerFn({\n  method: 'GET',\n}).handler(createClientRpc('sha256:deadbeef...'))\n\n// The server-only import is removed by the compiler.","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If the import \"leaks\" into code that survives compilation, it stays live and import protection will still flag it:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { getUsers } from './db/queries.server'\nimport { createServerFn } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\n// This is fine -- the server implementation is removed for the client build\nexport const fetchUsers = createServerFn().handler(async () => {\n  return getUsers()\n})\n\n// This keeps the import alive in the client build\nexport function leakyHelper() {\n  return getUsers() // referenced outside server boundary\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When this happens, you have a few options depending on what you want "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"leakyHelper"},{"type":"text","value":" to be:"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Option A: split the file so client code cannot accidentally import the leak"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// src/users.server.ts\nimport { getUsers } from './db/queries.server'\nimport { createServerFn } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\n// Safe to import from client code (compiler rewrites the handler)\nexport const fetchUsers = createServerFn().handler(async () => {\n  return getUsers()\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"code","value":"// src/users-leaky.server.ts\nimport { getUsers } from './db/queries.server'\n\n// Server-only helper; do not import this from client code\nexport function leakyHelper() {\n  return getUsers()\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Option B: keep it in the same file, but wrap the helper in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createServerOnlyFn"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This is useful when the helper should exist, but must never run on the client. Make sure the server-only import is only referenced inside the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createServerOnlyFn(() => ...)"},{"type":"text","value":" callback:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createServerOnlyFn } from '@tanstack/react-start'\nimport { getUsers } from './db/queries.server'\n\nexport const leakyHelper = createServerOnlyFn(() => {\n  return getUsers()\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On the client, the compiler output is effectively:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"export const leakyHelper = () => {\n  throw new Error(\n    'createServerOnlyFn() functions can only be called on the server!',\n  )\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Notice that the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createServerOnlyFn"},{"type":"text","value":" import is gone, and the server-only "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getUsers"},{"type":"text","value":" import is also gone because it is no longer referenced after compilation."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The same idea applies to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createIsomorphicFn()"},{"type":"text","value":": the compiler removes the non-target implementation and prunes anything that becomes unused."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you see an import-protection violation for a file you expected to be \"compiled away\", check whether the import is referenced outside a compiler-recognized environment boundary (or is otherwise kept live by surviving code)."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"false-positives-dev-vs-build","children":[{"type":"text","value":"False Positives: Dev vs Build"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"build mode"}]},{"type":"text","value":", import protection defers violation checks until after tree-shaking. If an import is eliminated from the final bundle, no violation is reported. Build-time violations are definitive: if the build flags it, the import truly survived."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"dev mode"}]},{"type":"text","value":", tree-shaking may be incomplete or unavailable, so import protection can report false positives for imports that would later be compiled away."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If a warning appears in dev but not build, that usually means the unsafe edge existed before tree-shaking but did not survive the final bundle. Even so, prefer changing the import structure so that edge does not exist in the first place."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"mixed-barrels-and-split-entry-points","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mixed Barrels and Split Entry Points"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Barrels are not inherently a false positive. The risky pattern is a barrel that mixes safe exports with environment-restricted ones from the same entry point."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Depending on what survives after compilation and tree-shaking, that can show up as either a real violation or a dev-only false positive. The safer structure is to split safe and restricted exports into separate entry points."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For example, avoid mixed barrels like this:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// src/lib/index.ts\nexport { fetchUsers } from './fetchUsers'\nexport { getDb } from './db.server'","lang":"ts"},{"type":"code","value":"// src/routes/users.tsx\nimport { fetchUsers } from '../lib'","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Even if the client only uses "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetchUsers"},{"type":"text","value":", that import path still goes through a module that re-exports "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getDb"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Prefer splitting safe and server-only exports into separate entry points:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// src/lib/index.ts\nexport { fetchUsers } from './fetchUsers'","lang":"ts"},{"type":"code","value":"// src/lib/server.ts\nexport { getDb } from './db.server'","lang":"ts"},{"type":"code","value":"// src/routes/users.tsx\nimport { fetchUsers } from '../lib'","lang":"ts"},{"type":"code","value":"// src/server/worker.ts\nimport { getDb } from '../lib/server'","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The same applies to marker-protected files ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"import '@tanstack/react-start/server-only'"},{"type":"text","value":"). If a marked file is re-exported through a mixed barrel but never consumed by client code, production may suppress the warning after tree-shaking, but the better fix is still to avoid exposing that server-only edge to client-reachable code at all."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"the-onviolation-callback","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onViolation"},{"type":"text","value":" Callback"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can hook into violations for custom reporting or to override the verdict:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"importProtection: {\n  onViolation: async (info) => {\n    // info.env -- environment name (e.g. 'client', 'ssr', ...)\n    // info.envType -- 'client' or 'server'\n    // info.type -- 'specifier', 'file', or 'marker'\n    // info.specifier -- the raw import string\n    // info.importer -- absolute path of the importing file\n    // info.resolved -- absolute path of the resolved target (if available)\n    // info.trace -- array of { file, line?, column?, specifier? } objects\n    // info.snippet -- { lines, highlightLine, location } with the source code snippet (if available)\n\n    // Return false (or Promise<false>) to allow this specific import (override the denial)\n    if (info.specifier === 'some-special-case') {\n      return false\n    }\n  },\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"disabling-import-protection","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Disabling Import Protection"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To disable import protection entirely:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"importProtection: {\n  enabled: false,\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"full-configuration-reference","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Full Configuration Reference"}]},{"type":"code","value":"interface ImportProtectionOptions {\n  enabled?: boolean\n  behavior?:\n    | 'error'\n    | 'mock'\n    | { dev?: 'error' | 'mock'; build?: 'error' | 'mock' }\n  log?: 'once' | 'always'\n  include?: Array<string | RegExp>\n  exclude?: Array<string | RegExp>\n  ignoreImporters?: Array<string | RegExp>\n  maxTraceDepth?: number\n  client?: {\n    specifiers?: Array<string | RegExp>\n    files?: Array<string | RegExp>\n    excludeFiles?: Array<string | RegExp>\n  }\n  server?: {\n    specifiers?: Array<string | RegExp>\n    files?: Array<string | RegExp>\n    excludeFiles?: Array<string | RegExp>\n  }\n  onViolation?: (\n    info: ViolationInfo,\n  ) => boolean | void | Promise<boolean | void>\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Option"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Type"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Default"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Description"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"enabled"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"boolean"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Set to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"},{"type":"text","value":" to disable the plugin"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"behavior"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"string | object"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ dev: 'mock', build: 'error' }"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What to do on violation"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"log"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'once' | 'always'"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'once'"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Whether to deduplicate repeated violations"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"include"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Pattern[]"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Start's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"srcDirectory"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Only check importers matching these patterns"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"exclude"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Pattern[]"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[]"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Skip importers matching these patterns"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ignoreImporters"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Pattern[]"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[]"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Ignore violations from these importers"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maxTraceDepth"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"number"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"20"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Maximum depth for import traces"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"client"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"object"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See defaults above"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Additional deny rules for the client environment"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"client.specifiers"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Pattern[]"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Framework server specifiers"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Specifier patterns denied in the client environment (additive with defaults)"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"client.files"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Pattern[]"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"['**/*.server.*']"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"File patterns denied in the client environment (replaces defaults)"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"client.excludeFiles"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Pattern[]"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"['**/node_modules/**']"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resolved files matching these patterns skip resolved-target checks (file-pattern + marker) (replaces defaults)"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"server"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"object"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See defaults above"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Additional deny rules for the server environment"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"server.specifiers"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Pattern[]"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[]"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Specifier patterns denied in the server environment (replaces defaults; defaults for "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"server.specifiers"},{"type":"text","value":" are "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[]"},{"type":"text","value":", so unlike "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"client.specifiers"},{"type":"text","value":" this isn't additive)"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"server.files"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Pattern[]"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"['**/*.client.*']"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"File patterns denied in the server environment (replaces defaults)"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"server.excludeFiles"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Pattern[]"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"['**/node_modules/**']"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resolved files matching these patterns skip resolved-target checks (file-pattern + marker) (replaces defaults)"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onViolation"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"function"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"undefined"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Callback invoked on every violation"}]}]]}],"headings":[{"id":"how-it-works","text":"How It Works","level":2},{"id":"default-rules","text":"Default Rules","level":2},{"id":"type-only-imports","text":"Type-Only Imports","level":2},{"id":"file-markers","text":"File Markers","level":2},{"id":"behavior-modes","text":"Behavior Modes","level":2},{"id":"configuring-deny-rules","text":"Configuring Deny Rules","level":2},{"id":"checking-third-party-packages","text":"Checking third-party packages","level":3},{"id":"scoping-and-exclusions","text":"Scoping and Exclusions","level":2},{"id":"reading-violation-traces","text":"Reading Violation Traces","level":2},{"id":"server-only-code-in-the-client","text":"Server-only code in the client","level":3},{"id":"client-only-code-on-the-server","text":"Client-only code on the server","level":3},{"id":"how-to-read-the-output","text":"How to read the output","level":3},{"id":"common-pitfall-why-some-imports-stay-alive","text":"Common Pitfall: Why Some Imports Stay Alive","level":2},{"id":"false-positives-dev-vs-build","text":"False Positives: Dev vs Build","level":2},{"id":"mixed-barrels-and-split-entry-points","text":"Mixed Barrels and Split Entry Points","level":2},{"id":"the-onviolation-callback","text":"The onViolation Callback","level":2},{"id":"disabling-import-protection","text":"Disabling Import Protection","level":2},{"id":"full-configuration-reference","text":"Full Configuration Reference","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\n> **Experimental:** Import protection is experimental and subject to change.\n\nImport protection prevents server-only code from leaking into client bundles and client-only code from leaking into server bundles. It runs inside TanStack Start and is enabled by default.\n\n## How It Works\n\nTanStack Start builds your application for two environments: **client** and **server**. Some code should only run in one environment. Import protection checks every import in your source files during development and build, and either blocks or mocks imports that cross environment boundaries.\n\nThere are two ways an import can be denied:\n\n- **File patterns** match on the resolved file path. By default, `*.server.*` files are denied in the client environment and `*.client.*` files are denied in the server environment.\n- **Specifier patterns** match on the raw import string. By default, `@tanstack/react-start/server` is denied in the client environment.\n\n## Default Rules\n\nImport protection is enabled out of the box with these defaults:\n\n| Setting            | Default                                         |\n| ------------------ | ----------------------------------------------- |\n| `behavior` (dev)   | `'mock'` -- warn and replace with a mock module |\n| `behavior` (build) | `'error'` -- fail the build                     |\n| `log`              | `'once'` -- deduplicate repeated violations     |\n| Scope              | Files inside Start's `srcDirectory`             |\n\n**Client environment denials:**\n\n- Files matching `**/*.server.*`\n- The specifier `@tanstack/react-start/server`\n- Excluded from file checks: `**/node_modules/**`\n\n**Server environment denials:**\n\n- Files matching `**/*.client.*`\n- Excluded from file checks: `**/node_modules/**`\n\nBy default, files inside `node_modules` are excluded from resolved-target deny checks via the `excludeFiles` option. This prevents false positives from third-party packages whose resolved filenames contain `.client.` or `.server.`. If you need to check third-party files, set `excludeFiles: []` on the relevant environment — see [Configuring Deny Rules](#configuring-deny-rules).\n\nThese defaults mean you can use the `.server.ts` / `.client.ts` naming convention to restrict files to a single environment without any configuration. To also deny entire directories (e.g. `server/` or `client/`), add them via `files` in your [deny rules configuration](#configuring-deny-rules) — for example `files: ['**/*.server.*', '**/server/**']` for the client environment.\n\n## Type-Only Imports\n\nType-only imports and re-exports are ignored by import protection because they are erased from the runtime bundle and cannot leak environment-specific code.\n\n```ts\nimport type { User } from './db.server'\nimport { type RequestHandler } from '@tanstack/react-start/server'\n\nexport type { User } from './db.server'\n```\n\nMixed imports still count when they include at least one runtime value. Split the type and value imports if only the type is safe to cross the environment boundary.\n\n```ts\n// This is still checked because `getUsers` is a runtime value.\nimport { type User, getUsers } from './db.server'\n```\n\n## File Markers\n\nYou can explicitly mark a module as server-only or client-only by adding a side-effect import at the top of the file:\n\n```ts\n// src/lib/secrets.ts\nimport '@tanstack/react-start/server-only'\n\nexport const API_KEY = process.env.API_KEY\n```\n\n```ts\n// src/lib/local-storage.ts\nimport '@tanstack/react-start/client-only'\n\nexport function savePreferences(prefs: Record<string, string>) {\n  localStorage.setItem('prefs', JSON.stringify(prefs))\n}\n```\n\nWhen the plugin sees a marker import, it records the file as restricted. If that file is later imported from the wrong environment, the import is denied. Both markers in the same file is always an error.\n\nMarkers are useful when a file doesn't follow the `.server.*` / `.client.*` naming convention but still contains environment-specific code.\n\n## Behavior Modes\n\nThe `behavior` option controls what happens when a violation is detected:\n\n- **`'error'`** -- The build fails with a detailed error message. This is the default for production builds.\n- **`'mock'`** -- The import is replaced with a mock module that returns safe proxy values. A warning is logged but the build continues. This is the default during development.\n\nMock mode is useful during development because it lets you keep working even when your import graph has violations. The mock module returns a recursive Proxy, so any property access or function call on a mocked import returns another mock instead of crashing.\n\nYou can override the defaults:\n\n<!-- ::start:tabs variant=\"bundler\" -->\n\n# Vite\n\n```ts title=\"vite.config.ts\"\nimport { defineConfig } from 'vite'\nimport { tanstackStart } from '@tanstack/react-start/plugin/vite'\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  plugins: [\n    tanstackStart({\n      importProtection: {\n        // Always error, even in dev\n        behavior: 'error',\n      },\n    }),\n  ],\n})\n```\n\n# Rsbuild\n\n```ts title=\"rsbuild.config.ts\"\nimport { defineConfig } from '@rsbuild/core'\nimport { tanstackStart } from '@tanstack/react-start/plugin/rsbuild'\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  plugins: [\n    tanstackStart({\n      importProtection: {\n        // Always error, even in dev\n        behavior: 'error',\n      },\n    }),\n  ],\n})\n```\n\n<!-- ::end:tabs -->\n\nOr set different behaviors per mode:\n\n```ts\nimportProtection: {\n  behavior: {\n    dev: 'mock',\n    build: 'error',\n  },\n}\n```\n\n## Configuring Deny Rules\n\nYou can add your own deny rules on top of the defaults. Rules are specified per environment using glob patterns (via [picomatch](https://github.com/micromatch/picomatch)) or regular expressions.\n\n<!-- ::start:tabs variant=\"bundler\" -->\n\n# Vite\n\n```ts title=\"vite.config.ts\"\nimport { defineConfig } from 'vite'\nimport { tanstackStart } from '@tanstack/react-start/plugin/vite'\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  plugins: [\n    tanstackStart({\n      importProtection: {\n        client: {\n          // Block specific npm packages from the client bundle\n          specifiers: ['@prisma/client', 'bcrypt'],\n          // Block files in a custom directory\n          files: ['**/db/**'],\n        },\n        server: {\n          // Block browser-only libraries from the server\n          specifiers: ['localforage'],\n        },\n      },\n    }),\n  ],\n})\n```\n\n# Rsbuild\n\n```ts title=\"rsbuild.config.ts\"\nimport { defineConfig } from '@rsbuild/core'\nimport { tanstackStart } from '@tanstack/react-start/plugin/rsbuild'\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  plugins: [\n    tanstackStart({\n      importProtection: {\n        client: {\n          // Block specific npm packages from the client bundle\n          specifiers: ['@prisma/client', 'bcrypt'],\n          // Block files in a custom directory\n          files: ['**/db/**'],\n        },\n        server: {\n          // Block browser-only libraries from the server\n          specifiers: ['localforage'],\n        },\n      },\n    }),\n  ],\n})\n```\n\n<!-- ::end:tabs -->\n\n### Checking third-party packages\n\nBy default, resolved files inside `node_modules` are excluded from resolved-target deny checks (file-pattern and marker checks). This avoids false positives from packages that happen to use `.client.` or `.server.` in their distribution filenames. If you want to re-enable checking for a specific environment, set `excludeFiles` to an empty array:\n\n```ts\nimportProtection: {\n  server: {\n    // Re-enable file-pattern checking for node_modules in the server environment\n    excludeFiles: [],\n  },\n}\n```\n\nWhen you provide `excludeFiles`, it **fully replaces** the default (`['**/node_modules/**']`). To exclude additional paths while still skipping `node_modules`, include both:\n\n```ts\nimportProtection: {\n  client: {\n    excludeFiles: ['**/node_modules/**', '**/vendor/**'],\n  },\n}\n```\n\n## Scoping and Exclusions\n\nBy default, import protection only checks files inside Start's `srcDirectory`. You can change the scope with `include`, `exclude`, and `ignoreImporters`:\n\n```ts\nimportProtection: {\n  // Only check files matching these patterns\n  include: ['src/**'],\n  // Skip checking these files\n  exclude: ['src/generated/**'],\n  // Ignore violations when these files are the importer\n  ignoreImporters: ['**/*.test.ts', '**/*.spec.ts'],\n}\n```\n\n## Reading Violation Traces\n\nWhen a violation is detected, the plugin shows a diagnostic message with the full import chain that led to the violation, a code snippet highlighting the offending line, and actionable suggestions.\n\n### Server-only code in the client\n\nThis example shows a `*.server.*` file being imported transitively in the client environment:\n\n```text\n[import-protection] Import denied in client environment\n\n  Denied by file pattern: **/*.server.*\n  Importer: src/features/auth/session.ts:5:27\n  Import: \"../db/queries.server\"\n  Resolved: src/db/queries.server.ts\n\n  Trace:\n    1. src/routes/index.tsx:2:34 (entry) (import \"../features/auth/session\")\n    2. src/features/auth/session.ts:5:27 (import \"../db/queries.server\")\n\n  Code:\n     3 | import { logger } from '../utils/logger'\n     4 |\n  >  5 | import { getUsers } from '../db/queries.server'\n       |                           ^\n     6 |\n     7 | export function loadAuth() {\n\n  src/features/auth/session.ts:5:27\n\n  Suggestions:\n    - Wrap in createServerFn().handler(() => ...) to make it callable from the client via RPC\n    - Wrap in createServerOnlyFn(() => ...) if it should not be callable from the client\n    - Use createIsomorphicFn().client(() => ...).server(() => ...) for environment-specific implementations\n    - Split the file so client-safe exports are separate\n```\n\n### Client-only code on the server\n\nThis example shows a `*.client.*` file imported in the SSR environment. Because the code snippet contains JSX, the `<ClientOnly>` suggestion is shown first:\n\n```text\n[import-protection] Import denied in server environment\n\n  Denied by file pattern: **/*.client.*\n  Importer: src/components/dashboard.tsx:3:30\n  Import: \"./browser-widget.client\"\n  Resolved: src/components/browser-widget.client.tsx\n\n  Trace:\n    1. src/routes/dashboard.tsx:1:32 (entry) (import \"../components/dashboard\")\n    2. src/components/dashboard.tsx:3:30 (import \"./browser-widget.client\")\n\n  Code:\n     1 | import { BrowserWidget } from './browser-widget.client'\n     2 |\n  >  3 | export function Dashboard() { return <BrowserWidget /> }\n       |                              ^\n     4 |\n\n  src/components/dashboard.tsx:3:30\n\n  Suggestions:\n    - Wrap in <ClientOnly fallback={...}>...</ClientOnly> to render only after hydration\n    - Wrap in createClientOnlyFn(() => ...) if it should only run in the browser\n    - Use createIsomorphicFn().client(() => ...).server(() => ...) for environment-specific implementations\n    - Split the file so server-safe exports are separate\n```\n\n### How to read the output\n\nEach violation message has these sections:\n\n| Section                          | Description                                                                                                                                                      |\n| -------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Header**                       | Environment type where the violation occurred (`\"client\"` or `\"server\"`)                                                                                         |\n| **Denied by**                    | The rule that matched: file pattern, specifier pattern, or marker                                                                                                |\n| **Importer / Import / Resolved** | The importing file (with `file:line:col`), the raw import string, and the resolved target path                                                                   |\n| **Trace**                        | The full import chain from the entry point to the denied import. Each step shows `file:line:col` and the import specifier used. Step 1 is always the entry point |\n| **Code**                         | A source code snippet with a `>` marker on the offending line and a `^` caret pointing to the exact column                                                       |\n| **Suggestions**                  | Actionable steps to fix the violation, tailored to the direction (server-in-client vs client-in-server)                                                          |\n\nThe trace reads top-to-bottom, from the entry point to the denied module. This helps you find where the chain starts so you can restructure your code.\n\n## Common Pitfall: Why Some Imports Stay Alive\n\nIt can look like Start \"should have removed that server-only import\". The important detail is that this is handled by the Start compiler:\n\n1. The compiler rewrites environment-specific _implementations_ for the current target (client or server).\n2. As part of that compilation, it prunes code and removes imports that become unused after the rewrite.\n\nIn practice, when the compiler replaces a `createServerFn()` handler with a client RPC stub, it can also remove server-only imports that were only used by the removed implementation.\n\nExample (client build):\n\n```ts\nimport { getUsers } from './db/queries.server'\nimport { createServerFn } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nexport const fetchUsers = createServerFn().handler(async () => {\n  return getUsers()\n})\n```\n\nConceptually, the client build output becomes something like (simplified):\n\n```ts\nimport { createClientRpc } from '@tanstack/react-start/client-rpc'\nimport { createServerFn } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\n// Compiler replaces the handler with a client RPC stub.\n// (The id is generated by the compiler; treat it as an opaque identifier.)\nexport const fetchUsers = TanStackStart.createServerFn({\n  method: 'GET',\n}).handler(createClientRpc('sha256:deadbeef...'))\n\n// The server-only import is removed by the compiler.\n```\n\nIf the import \"leaks\" into code that survives compilation, it stays live and import protection will still flag it:\n\n```ts\nimport { getUsers } from './db/queries.server'\nimport { createServerFn } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\n// This is fine -- the server implementation is removed for the client build\nexport const fetchUsers = createServerFn().handler(async () => {\n  return getUsers()\n})\n\n// This keeps the import alive in the client build\nexport function leakyHelper() {\n  return getUsers() // referenced outside server boundary\n}\n```\n\nWhen this happens, you have a few options depending on what you want `leakyHelper` to be:\n\nOption A: split the file so client code cannot accidentally import the leak\n\n```ts\n// src/users.server.ts\nimport { getUsers } from './db/queries.server'\nimport { createServerFn } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\n// Safe to import from client code (compiler rewrites the handler)\nexport const fetchUsers = createServerFn().handler(async () => {\n  return getUsers()\n})\n```\n\n```ts\n// src/users-leaky.server.ts\nimport { getUsers } from './db/queries.server'\n\n// Server-only helper; do not import this from client code\nexport function leakyHelper() {\n  return getUsers()\n}\n```\n\nOption B: keep it in the same file, but wrap the helper in `createServerOnlyFn`\n\nThis is useful when the helper should exist, but must never run on the client. Make sure the server-only import is only referenced inside the `createServerOnlyFn(() => ...)` callback:\n\n```ts\nimport { createServerOnlyFn } from '@tanstack/react-start'\nimport { getUsers } from './db/queries.server'\n\nexport const leakyHelper = createServerOnlyFn(() => {\n  return getUsers()\n})\n```\n\nOn the client, the compiler output is effectively:\n\n```ts\nexport const leakyHelper = () => {\n  throw new Error(\n    'createServerOnlyFn() functions can only be called on the server!',\n  )\n}\n```\n\nNotice that the `createServerOnlyFn` import is gone, and the server-only `getUsers` import is also gone because it is no longer referenced after compilation.\n\nThe same idea applies to `createIsomorphicFn()`: the compiler removes the non-target implementation and prunes anything that becomes unused.\n\nIf you see an import-protection violation for a file you expected to be \"compiled away\", check whether the import is referenced outside a compiler-recognized environment boundary (or is otherwise kept live by surviving code).\n\n## False Positives: Dev vs Build\n\nIn **build mode**, import protection defers violation checks until after tree-shaking. If an import is eliminated from the final bundle, no violation is reported. Build-time violations are definitive: if the build flags it, the import truly survived.\n\nIn **dev mode**, tree-shaking may be incomplete or unavailable, so import protection can report false positives for imports that would later be compiled away.\n\nIf a warning appears in dev but not build, that usually means the unsafe edge existed before tree-shaking but did not survive the final bundle. Even so, prefer changing the import structure so that edge does not exist in the first place.\n\n## Mixed Barrels and Split Entry Points\n\nBarrels are not inherently a false positive. The risky pattern is a barrel that mixes safe exports with environment-restricted ones from the same entry point.\n\nDepending on what survives after compilation and tree-shaking, that can show up as either a real violation or a dev-only false positive. The safer structure is to split safe and restricted exports into separate entry points.\n\nFor example, avoid mixed barrels like this:\n\n```ts\n// src/lib/index.ts\nexport { fetchUsers } from './fetchUsers'\nexport { getDb } from './db.server'\n```\n\n```ts\n// src/routes/users.tsx\nimport { fetchUsers } from '../lib'\n```\n\nEven if the client only uses `fetchUsers`, that import path still goes through a module that re-exports `getDb`.\n\nPrefer splitting safe and server-only exports into separate entry points:\n\n```ts\n// src/lib/index.ts\nexport { fetchUsers } from './fetchUsers'\n```\n\n```ts\n// src/lib/server.ts\nexport { getDb } from './db.server'\n```\n\n```ts\n// src/routes/users.tsx\nimport { fetchUsers } from '../lib'\n```\n\n```ts\n// src/server/worker.ts\nimport { getDb } from '../lib/server'\n```\n\nThe same applies to marker-protected files (`import '@tanstack/react-start/server-only'`). If a marked file is re-exported through a mixed barrel but never consumed by client code, production may suppress the warning after tree-shaking, but the better fix is still to avoid exposing that server-only edge to client-reachable code at all.\n\n## The `onViolation` Callback\n\nYou can hook into violations for custom reporting or to override the verdict:\n\n```ts\nimportProtection: {\n  onViolation: async (info) => {\n    // info.env -- environment name (e.g. 'client', 'ssr', ...)\n    // info.envType -- 'client' or 'server'\n    // info.type -- 'specifier', 'file', or 'marker'\n    // info.specifier -- the raw import string\n    // info.importer -- absolute path of the importing file\n    // info.resolved -- absolute path of the resolved target (if available)\n    // info.trace -- array of { file, line?, column?, specifier? } objects\n    // info.snippet -- { lines, highlightLine, location } with the source code snippet (if available)\n\n    // Return false (or Promise<false>) to allow this specific import (override the denial)\n    if (info.specifier === 'some-special-case') {\n      return false\n    }\n  },\n}\n```\n\n## Disabling Import Protection\n\nTo disable import protection entirely:\n\n```ts\nimportProtection: {\n  enabled: false,\n}\n```\n\n## Full Configuration Reference\n\n```ts\ninterface ImportProtectionOptions {\n  enabled?: boolean\n  behavior?:\n    | 'error'\n    | 'mock'\n    | { dev?: 'error' | 'mock'; build?: 'error' | 'mock' }\n  log?: 'once' | 'always'\n  include?: Array<string | RegExp>\n  exclude?: Array<string | RegExp>\n  ignoreImporters?: Array<string | RegExp>\n  maxTraceDepth?: number\n  client?: {\n    specifiers?: Array<string | RegExp>\n    files?: Array<string | RegExp>\n    excludeFiles?: Array<string | RegExp>\n  }\n  server?: {\n    specifiers?: Array<string | RegExp>\n    files?: Array<string | RegExp>\n    excludeFiles?: Array<string | RegExp>\n  }\n  onViolation?: (\n    info: ViolationInfo,\n  ) => boolean | void | Promise<boolean | void>\n}\n```\n\n| Option                | Type                 | Default                           | Description                                                                                                                                                           |\n| --------------------- | -------------------- | --------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `enabled`             | `boolean`            | `true`                            | Set to `false` to disable the plugin                                                                                                                                  |\n| `behavior`            | `string \\| object`   | `{ dev: 'mock', build: 'error' }` | What to do on violation                                                                                                                                               |\n| `log`                 | `'once' \\| 'always'` | `'once'`                          | Whether to deduplicate repeated violations                                                                                                                            |\n| `include`             | `Pattern[]`          | Start's `srcDirectory`            | Only check importers matching these patterns                                                                                                                          |\n| `exclude`             | `Pattern[]`          | `[]`                              | Skip importers matching these patterns                                                                                                                                |\n| `ignoreImporters`     | `Pattern[]`          | `[]`                              | Ignore violations from these importers                                                                                                                                |\n| `maxTraceDepth`       | `number`             | `20`                              | Maximum depth for import traces                                                                                                                                       |\n| `client`              | `object`             | See defaults above                | Additional deny rules for the client environment                                                                                                                      |\n| `client.specifiers`   | `Pattern[]`          | Framework server specifiers       | Specifier patterns denied in the client environment (additive with defaults)                                                                                          |\n| `client.files`        | `Pattern[]`          | `['**/*.server.*']`               | File patterns denied in the client environment (replaces defaults)                                                                                                    |\n| `client.excludeFiles` | `Pattern[]`          | `['**/node_modules/**']`          | Resolved files matching these patterns skip resolved-target checks (file-pattern + marker) (replaces defaults)                                                        |\n| `server`              | `object`             | See defaults above                | Additional deny rules for the server environment                                                                                                                      |\n| `server.specifiers`   | `Pattern[]`          | `[]`                              | Specifier patterns denied in the server environment (replaces defaults; defaults for `server.specifiers` are `[]`, so unlike `client.specifiers` this isn't additive) |\n| `server.files`        | `Pattern[]`          | `['**/*.client.*']`               | File patterns denied in the server environment (replaces defaults)                                                                                                    |\n| `server.excludeFiles` | `Pattern[]`          | `['**/node_modules/**']`          | Resolved files matching these patterns skip resolved-target checks (file-pattern + marker) (replaces defaults)                                                        |\n| `onViolation`         | `function`           | `undefined`                       | Callback invoked on every violation                                                                                                                                   |\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\nid: import-protection\ntitle: Import Protection\n---\n\n> **Experimental:** Import protection is experimental and subject to change.\n\nImport protection prevents server-only code from leaking into client bundles and client-only code from leaking into server bundles. It runs inside TanStack Start and is enabled by default.\n\n## How It Works\n\nTanStack Start builds your application for two environments: **client** and **server**. Some code should only run in one environment. Import protection checks every import in your source files during development and build, and either blocks or mocks imports that cross environment boundaries.\n\nThere are two ways an import can be denied:\n\n- **File patterns** match on the resolved file path. By default, `*.server.*` files are denied in the client environment and `*.client.*` files are denied in the server environment.\n- **Specifier patterns** match on the raw import string. By default, `@tanstack/react-start/server` is denied in the client environment.\n\n## Default Rules\n\nImport protection is enabled out of the box with these defaults:\n\n| Setting            | Default                                         |\n| ------------------ | ----------------------------------------------- |\n| `behavior` (dev)   | `'mock'` -- warn and replace with a mock module |\n| `behavior` (build) | `'error'` -- fail the build                     |\n| `log`              | `'once'` -- deduplicate repeated violations     |\n| Scope              | Files inside Start's `srcDirectory`             |\n\n**Client environment denials:**\n\n- Files matching `**/*.server.*`\n- The specifier `@tanstack/react-start/server`\n- Excluded from file checks: `**/node_modules/**`\n\n**Server environment denials:**\n\n- Files matching `**/*.client.*`\n- Excluded from file checks: `**/node_modules/**`\n\nBy default, files inside `node_modules` are excluded from resolved-target deny checks via the `excludeFiles` option. This prevents false positives from third-party packages whose resolved filenames contain `.client.` or `.server.`. If you need to check third-party files, set `excludeFiles: []` on the relevant environment — see [Configuring Deny Rules](#configuring-deny-rules).\n\nThese defaults mean you can use the `.server.ts` / `.client.ts` naming convention to restrict files to a single environment without any configuration. To also deny entire directories (e.g. `server/` or `client/`), add them via `files` in your [deny rules configuration](#configuring-deny-rules) — for example `files: ['**/*.server.*', '**/server/**']` for the client environment.\n\n## Type-Only Imports\n\nType-only imports and re-exports are ignored by import protection because they are erased from the runtime bundle and cannot leak environment-specific code.\n\n```ts\nimport type { User } from './db.server'\nimport { type RequestHandler } from '@tanstack/react-start/server'\n\nexport type { User } from './db.server'\n```\n\nMixed imports still count when they include at least one runtime value. Split the type and value imports if only the type is safe to cross the environment boundary.\n\n```ts\n// This is still checked because `getUsers` is a runtime value.\nimport { type User, getUsers } from './db.server'\n```\n\n## File Markers\n\nYou can explicitly mark a module as server-only or client-only by adding a side-effect import at the top of the file:\n\n```ts\n// src/lib/secrets.ts\nimport '@tanstack/react-start/server-only'\n\nexport const API_KEY = process.env.API_KEY\n```\n\n```ts\n// src/lib/local-storage.ts\nimport '@tanstack/react-start/client-only'\n\nexport function savePreferences(prefs: Record<string, string>) {\n  localStorage.setItem('prefs', JSON.stringify(prefs))\n}\n```\n\nWhen the plugin sees a marker import, it records the file as restricted. If that file is later imported from the wrong environment, the import is denied. Both markers in the same file is always an error.\n\nMarkers are useful when a file doesn't follow the `.server.*` / `.client.*` naming convention but still contains environment-specific code.\n\n## Behavior Modes\n\nThe `behavior` option controls what happens when a violation is detected:\n\n- **`'error'`** -- The build fails with a detailed error message. This is the default for production builds.\n- **`'mock'`** -- The import is replaced with a mock module that returns safe proxy values. A warning is logged but the build continues. This is the default during development.\n\nMock mode is useful during development because it lets you keep working even when your import graph has violations. The mock module returns a recursive Proxy, so any property access or function call on a mocked import returns another mock instead of crashing.\n\nYou can override the defaults:\n\n<!-- ::start:tabs variant=\"bundler\" -->\n\n# Vite\n\n```ts title=\"vite.config.ts\"\nimport { defineConfig } from 'vite'\nimport { tanstackStart } from '@tanstack/react-start/plugin/vite'\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  plugins: [\n    tanstackStart({\n      importProtection: {\n        // Always error, even in dev\n        behavior: 'error',\n      },\n    }),\n  ],\n})\n```\n\n# Rsbuild\n\n```ts title=\"rsbuild.config.ts\"\nimport { defineConfig } from '@rsbuild/core'\nimport { tanstackStart } from '@tanstack/react-start/plugin/rsbuild'\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  plugins: [\n    tanstackStart({\n      importProtection: {\n        // Always error, even in dev\n        behavior: 'error',\n      },\n    }),\n  ],\n})\n```\n\n<!-- ::end:tabs -->\n\nOr set different behaviors per mode:\n\n```ts\nimportProtection: {\n  behavior: {\n    dev: 'mock',\n    build: 'error',\n  },\n}\n```\n\n## Configuring Deny Rules\n\nYou can add your own deny rules on top of the defaults. Rules are specified per environment using glob patterns (via [picomatch](https://github.com/micromatch/picomatch)) or regular expressions.\n\n<!-- ::start:tabs variant=\"bundler\" -->\n\n# Vite\n\n```ts title=\"vite.config.ts\"\nimport { defineConfig } from 'vite'\nimport { tanstackStart } from '@tanstack/react-start/plugin/vite'\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  plugins: [\n    tanstackStart({\n      importProtection: {\n        client: {\n          // Block specific npm packages from the client bundle\n          specifiers: ['@prisma/client', 'bcrypt'],\n          // Block files in a custom directory\n          files: ['**/db/**'],\n        },\n        server: {\n          // Block browser-only libraries from the server\n          specifiers: ['localforage'],\n        },\n      },\n    }),\n  ],\n})\n```\n\n# Rsbuild\n\n```ts title=\"rsbuild.config.ts\"\nimport { defineConfig } from '@rsbuild/core'\nimport { tanstackStart } from '@tanstack/react-start/plugin/rsbuild'\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  plugins: [\n    tanstackStart({\n      importProtection: {\n        client: {\n          // Block specific npm packages from the client bundle\n          specifiers: ['@prisma/client', 'bcrypt'],\n          // Block files in a custom directory\n          files: ['**/db/**'],\n        },\n        server: {\n          // Block browser-only libraries from the server\n          specifiers: ['localforage'],\n        },\n      },\n    }),\n  ],\n})\n```\n\n<!-- ::end:tabs -->\n\n### Checking third-party packages\n\nBy default, resolved files inside `node_modules` are excluded from resolved-target deny checks (file-pattern and marker checks). This avoids false positives from packages that happen to use `.client.` or `.server.` in their distribution filenames. If you want to re-enable checking for a specific environment, set `excludeFiles` to an empty array:\n\n```ts\nimportProtection: {\n  server: {\n    // Re-enable file-pattern checking for node_modules in the server environment\n    excludeFiles: [],\n  },\n}\n```\n\nWhen you provide `excludeFiles`, it **fully replaces** the default (`['**/node_modules/**']`). To exclude additional paths while still skipping `node_modules`, include both:\n\n```ts\nimportProtection: {\n  client: {\n    excludeFiles: ['**/node_modules/**', '**/vendor/**'],\n  },\n}\n```\n\n## Scoping and Exclusions\n\nBy default, import protection only checks files inside Start's `srcDirectory`. You can change the scope with `include`, `exclude`, and `ignoreImporters`:\n\n```ts\nimportProtection: {\n  // Only check files matching these patterns\n  include: ['src/**'],\n  // Skip checking these files\n  exclude: ['src/generated/**'],\n  // Ignore violations when these files are the importer\n  ignoreImporters: ['**/*.test.ts', '**/*.spec.ts'],\n}\n```\n\n## Reading Violation Traces\n\nWhen a violation is detected, the plugin shows a diagnostic message with the full import chain that led to the violation, a code snippet highlighting the offending line, and actionable suggestions.\n\n### Server-only code in the client\n\nThis example shows a `*.server.*` file being imported transitively in the client environment:\n\n```text\n[import-protection] Import denied in client environment\n\n  Denied by file pattern: **/*.server.*\n  Importer: src/features/auth/session.ts:5:27\n  Import: \"../db/queries.server\"\n  Resolved: src/db/queries.server.ts\n\n  Trace:\n    1. src/routes/index.tsx:2:34 (entry) (import \"../features/auth/session\")\n    2. src/features/auth/session.ts:5:27 (import \"../db/queries.server\")\n\n  Code:\n     3 | import { logger } from '../utils/logger'\n     4 |\n  >  5 | import { getUsers } from '../db/queries.server'\n       |                           ^\n     6 |\n     7 | export function loadAuth() {\n\n  src/features/auth/session.ts:5:27\n\n  Suggestions:\n    - Wrap in createServerFn().handler(() => ...) to make it callable from the client via RPC\n    - Wrap in createServerOnlyFn(() => ...) if it should not be callable from the client\n    - Use createIsomorphicFn().client(() => ...).server(() => ...) for environment-specific implementations\n    - Split the file so client-safe exports are separate\n```\n\n### Client-only code on the server\n\nThis example shows a `*.client.*` file imported in the SSR environment. Because the code snippet contains JSX, the `<ClientOnly>` suggestion is shown first:\n\n```text\n[import-protection] Import denied in server environment\n\n  Denied by file pattern: **/*.client.*\n  Importer: src/components/dashboard.tsx:3:30\n  Import: \"./browser-widget.client\"\n  Resolved: src/components/browser-widget.client.tsx\n\n  Trace:\n    1. src/routes/dashboard.tsx:1:32 (entry) (import \"../components/dashboard\")\n    2. src/components/dashboard.tsx:3:30 (import \"./browser-widget.client\")\n\n  Code:\n     1 | import { BrowserWidget } from './browser-widget.client'\n     2 |\n  >  3 | export function Dashboard() { return <BrowserWidget /> }\n       |                              ^\n     4 |\n\n  src/components/dashboard.tsx:3:30\n\n  Suggestions:\n    - Wrap in <ClientOnly fallback={...}>...</ClientOnly> to render only after hydration\n    - Wrap in createClientOnlyFn(() => ...) if it should only run in the browser\n    - Use createIsomorphicFn().client(() => ...).server(() => ...) for environment-specific implementations\n    - Split the file so server-safe exports are separate\n```\n\n### How to read the output\n\nEach violation message has these sections:\n\n| Section                          | Description                                                                                                                                                      |\n| -------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Header**                       | Environment type where the violation occurred (`\"client\"` or `\"server\"`)                                                                                         |\n| **Denied by**                    | The rule that matched: file pattern, specifier pattern, or marker                                                                                                |\n| **Importer / Import / Resolved** | The importing file (with `file:line:col`), the raw import string, and the resolved target path                                                                   |\n| **Trace**                        | The full import chain from the entry point to the denied import. Each step shows `file:line:col` and the import specifier used. Step 1 is always the entry point |\n| **Code**                         | A source code snippet with a `>` marker on the offending line and a `^` caret pointing to the exact column                                                       |\n| **Suggestions**                  | Actionable steps to fix the violation, tailored to the direction (server-in-client vs client-in-server)                                                          |\n\nThe trace reads top-to-bottom, from the entry point to the denied module. This helps you find where the chain starts so you can restructure your code.\n\n## Common Pitfall: Why Some Imports Stay Alive\n\nIt can look like Start \"should have removed that server-only import\". The important detail is that this is handled by the Start compiler:\n\n1. The compiler rewrites environment-specific _implementations_ for the current target (client or server).\n2. As part of that compilation, it prunes code and removes imports that become unused after the rewrite.\n\nIn practice, when the compiler replaces a `createServerFn()` handler with a client RPC stub, it can also remove server-only imports that were only used by the removed implementation.\n\nExample (client build):\n\n```ts\nimport { getUsers } from './db/queries.server'\nimport { createServerFn } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\nexport const fetchUsers = createServerFn().handler(async () => {\n  return getUsers()\n})\n```\n\nConceptually, the client build output becomes something like (simplified):\n\n```ts\nimport { createClientRpc } from '@tanstack/react-start/client-rpc'\nimport { createServerFn } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\n// Compiler replaces the handler with a client RPC stub.\n// (The id is generated by the compiler; treat it as an opaque identifier.)\nexport const fetchUsers = TanStackStart.createServerFn({\n  method: 'GET',\n}).handler(createClientRpc('sha256:deadbeef...'))\n\n// The server-only import is removed by the compiler.\n```\n\nIf the import \"leaks\" into code that survives compilation, it stays live and import protection will still flag it:\n\n```ts\nimport { getUsers } from './db/queries.server'\nimport { createServerFn } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\n// This is fine -- the server implementation is removed for the client build\nexport const fetchUsers = createServerFn().handler(async () => {\n  return getUsers()\n})\n\n// This keeps the import alive in the client build\nexport function leakyHelper() {\n  return getUsers() // referenced outside server boundary\n}\n```\n\nWhen this happens, you have a few options depending on what you want `leakyHelper` to be:\n\nOption A: split the file so client code cannot accidentally import the leak\n\n```ts\n// src/users.server.ts\nimport { getUsers } from './db/queries.server'\nimport { createServerFn } from '@tanstack/react-start'\n\n// Safe to import from client code (compiler rewrites the handler)\nexport const fetchUsers = createServerFn().handler(async () => {\n  return getUsers()\n})\n```\n\n```ts\n// src/users-leaky.server.ts\nimport { getUsers } from './db/queries.server'\n\n// Server-only helper; do not import this from client code\nexport function leakyHelper() {\n  return getUsers()\n}\n```\n\nOption B: keep it in the same file, but wrap the helper in `createServerOnlyFn`\n\nThis is useful when the helper should exist, but must never run on the client. Make sure the server-only import is only referenced inside the `createServerOnlyFn(() => ...)` callback:\n\n```ts\nimport { createServerOnlyFn } from '@tanstack/react-start'\nimport { getUsers } from './db/queries.server'\n\nexport const leakyHelper = createServerOnlyFn(() => {\n  return getUsers()\n})\n```\n\nOn the client, the compiler output is effectively:\n\n```ts\nexport const leakyHelper = () => {\n  throw new Error(\n    'createServerOnlyFn() functions can only be called on the server!',\n  )\n}\n```\n\nNotice that the `createServerOnlyFn` import is gone, and the server-only `getUsers` import is also gone because it is no longer referenced after compilation.\n\nThe same idea applies to `createIsomorphicFn()`: the compiler removes the non-target implementation and prunes anything that becomes unused.\n\nIf you see an import-protection violation for a file you expected to be \"compiled away\", check whether the import is referenced outside a compiler-recognized environment boundary (or is otherwise kept live by surviving code).\n\n## False Positives: Dev vs Build\n\nIn **build mode**, import protection defers violation checks until after tree-shaking. If an import is eliminated from the final bundle, no violation is reported. Build-time violations are definitive: if the build flags it, the import truly survived.\n\nIn **dev mode**, tree-shaking may be incomplete or unavailable, so import protection can report false positives for imports that would later be compiled away.\n\nIf a warning appears in dev but not build, that usually means the unsafe edge existed before tree-shaking but did not survive the final bundle. Even so, prefer changing the import structure so that edge does not exist in the first place.\n\n## Mixed Barrels and Split Entry Points\n\nBarrels are not inherently a false positive. The risky pattern is a barrel that mixes safe exports with environment-restricted ones from the same entry point.\n\nDepending on what survives after compilation and tree-shaking, that can show up as either a real violation or a dev-only false positive. The safer structure is to split safe and restricted exports into separate entry points.\n\nFor example, avoid mixed barrels like this:\n\n```ts\n// src/lib/index.ts\nexport { fetchUsers } from './fetchUsers'\nexport { getDb } from './db.server'\n```\n\n```ts\n// src/routes/users.tsx\nimport { fetchUsers } from '../lib'\n```\n\nEven if the client only uses `fetchUsers`, that import path still goes through a module that re-exports `getDb`.\n\nPrefer splitting safe and server-only exports into separate entry points:\n\n```ts\n// src/lib/index.ts\nexport { fetchUsers } from './fetchUsers'\n```\n\n```ts\n// src/lib/server.ts\nexport { getDb } from './db.server'\n```\n\n```ts\n// src/routes/users.tsx\nimport { fetchUsers } from '../lib'\n```\n\n```ts\n// src/server/worker.ts\nimport { getDb } from '../lib/server'\n```\n\nThe same applies to marker-protected files (`import '@tanstack/react-start/server-only'`). If a marked file is re-exported through a mixed barrel but never consumed by client code, production may suppress the warning after tree-shaking, but the better fix is still to avoid exposing that server-only edge to client-reachable code at all.\n\n## The `onViolation` Callback\n\nYou can hook into violations for custom reporting or to override the verdict:\n\n```ts\nimportProtection: {\n  onViolation: async (info) => {\n    // info.env -- environment name (e.g. 'client', 'ssr', ...)\n    // info.envType -- 'client' or 'server'\n    // info.type -- 'specifier', 'file', or 'marker'\n    // info.specifier -- the raw import string\n    // info.importer -- absolute path of the importing file\n    // info.resolved -- absolute path of the resolved target (if available)\n    // info.trace -- array of { file, line?, column?, specifier? } objects\n    // info.snippet -- { lines, highlightLine, location } with the source code snippet (if available)\n\n    // Return false (or Promise<false>) to allow this specific import (override the denial)\n    if (info.specifier === 'some-special-case') {\n      return false\n    }\n  },\n}\n```\n\n## Disabling Import Protection\n\nTo disable import protection entirely:\n\n```ts\nimportProtection: {\n  enabled: false,\n}\n```\n\n## Full Configuration Reference\n\n```ts\ninterface ImportProtectionOptions {\n  enabled?: boolean\n  behavior?:\n    | 'error'\n    | 'mock'\n    | { dev?: 'error' | 'mock'; build?: 'error' | 'mock' }\n  log?: 'once' | 'always'\n  include?: Array<string | RegExp>\n  exclude?: Array<string | RegExp>\n  ignoreImporters?: Array<string | RegExp>\n  maxTraceDepth?: number\n  client?: {\n    specifiers?: Array<string | RegExp>\n    files?: Array<string | RegExp>\n    excludeFiles?: Array<string | RegExp>\n  }\n  server?: {\n    specifiers?: Array<string | RegExp>\n    files?: Array<string | RegExp>\n    excludeFiles?: Array<string | RegExp>\n  }\n  onViolation?: (\n    info: ViolationInfo,\n  ) => boolean | void | Promise<boolean | void>\n}\n```\n\n| Option                | Type                 | Default                           | Description                                                                                                                                                           |\n| --------------------- | -------------------- | --------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `enabled`             | `boolean`            | `true`                            | Set to `false` to disable the plugin                                                                                                                                  |\n| `behavior`            | `string \\| object`   | `{ dev: 'mock', build: 'error' }` | What to do on violation                                                                                                                                               |\n| `log`                 | `'once' \\| 'always'` | `'once'`                          | Whether to deduplicate repeated violations                                                                                                                            |\n| `include`             | `Pattern[]`          | Start's `srcDirectory`            | Only check importers matching these patterns                                                                                                                          |\n| `exclude`             | `Pattern[]`          | `[]`                              | Skip importers matching these patterns                                                                                                                                |\n| `ignoreImporters`     | `Pattern[]`          | `[]`                              | Ignore violations from these importers                                                                                                                                |\n| `maxTraceDepth`       | `number`             | `20`                              | Maximum depth for import traces                                                                                                                                       |\n| `client`              | `object`             | See defaults above                | Additional deny rules for the client environment                                                                                                                      |\n| `client.specifiers`   | `Pattern[]`          | Framework server specifiers       | Specifier patterns denied in the client environment (additive with defaults)                                                                                          |\n| `client.files`        | `Pattern[]`          | `['**/*.server.*']`               | File patterns denied in the client environment (replaces defaults)                                                                                                    |\n| `client.excludeFiles` | `Pattern[]`          | `['**/node_modules/**']`          | Resolved files matching these patterns skip resolved-target checks (file-pattern + marker) (replaces defaults)                                                        |\n| `server`              | `object`             | See defaults above                | Additional deny rules for the server environment                                                                                                                      |\n| `server.specifiers`   | `Pattern[]`          | `[]`                              | Specifier patterns denied in the server environment (replaces defaults; defaults for `server.specifiers` are `[]`, so unlike `client.specifiers` this isn't additive) |\n| `server.files`        | `Pattern[]`          | `['**/*.client.*']`               | File patterns denied in the server environment (replaces defaults)                                                                                                    |\n| `server.excludeFiles` | `Pattern[]`          | `['**/node_modules/**']`          | Resolved files matching these patterns skip resolved-target checks (file-pattern + marker) (replaces defaults)                                                        |\n| `onViolation`         | `function`           | `undefined`                       | Callback invoked on every violation                                                                                                                                   |\n"}
